# Project Rules — Apply to Every Task

## 1. Mobile-First is the #1 Priority
- Design and build for mobile screens (320px–430px) FIRST
- Only then scale up to tablet (768px) and desktop (1024px+)
- Use Tailwind's mobile-first breakpoints: base styles are mobile, use `md:` and `lg:` to override for larger screens
- Never write desktop styles first and try to shrink them down

## 2. Performance & Loading Speed
- Always use Next.js `<Image />` component instead of plain `<img>` tags — it handles lazy loading and compression automatically
- Never import large libraries if a small utility function can do the job
- Avoid unnecessary animations or heavy effects on mobile
- Keep font usage minimal — use only the fonts already in the project

## 3. Follow the Existing Design System
- Before adding any new color, spacing, font size, or component style — check what already exists in the codebase first
- Match the existing color palette, typography scale, and spacing values exactly
- Use Tailwind utility classes only — no inline styles, no separate CSS files unless one already exists in the project
- If a component already exists, reuse it. Don't create duplicates.

## 4. Touch-Friendly UI
- All buttons and clickable elements must be at least 44px tall (use `min-h-[44px]`)
- Add enough padding so elements are easy to tap with a thumb
- Avoid hover-only interactions — mobile users cannot hover

## 5. Responsive Layout Rules
- Use `flex` and `grid` layouts that stack vertically on mobile and go side-by-side on larger screens
- Images must never overflow their container — always use `w-full` or constrain with a max-width
- Text must be readable without zooming — minimum `text-base` (16px) for body text

## 6. General Code Quality
- Write clean, readable code with clear component names
- Add a short comment if a block of code is doing something non-obvious
- Don't break existing functionality when adding new features